Persistent inference checkpoints (V6/V11 stage B): feasibility, decision and prototype. Qwen3.5-4B-Q8 on the RTX 5050
laptop GPU (8 GB, 40 W), Ryzen 7 260, 16 GB RAM, WD SN7100 NVMe. Release builds. Text resume stays the fallback.

1. What a restore must bring back (crates/model/src/qwen35/gpu_state.rs)
   - DeltaNet: 24 layers x (convolution 8192x4 f32 = 128 KiB + recurrent 32x128x128 f32 = 2 MiB) = 51 MiB, fixed.
   - Attention: 8 full-attention layers x (K,V) x 4 kv heads x 256 x f32 = 64 KiB per token, in 64-token pages.
   - The token list (4 bytes per token) and nothing else: position = number of tokens, so no counters to restore.
   - Checks before use: format version, session name and revision, mode, model name, hash of config+tokenizer, hash of
     the weight files, weight bits and group, the state layout, token count against the context, file size, a hash of the
     header, of the tokens and of every block, the end mark, and that the tokens begin the conversation's history.
   - Size in bytes: 51 MiB + 64 KiB x tokens (+ 4 x tokens). Measured files: 495 tokens 86 MB, 1,382 144 MB,
     2,265 202 MB, 472 84 MB.

2. Cold replay of history (marin-run bench ... --prompt-tokens N --tokens 4 --ctx N+64 --runs 1 --warmup 1; load 3.05-3.30 s)
     tokens   first token (replay)   prefill     kv held    gpu peak
        256        1.87 s            137.1 tok/s   87 MB     5.23 GB
        512        3.82 s            134.2         121 MB    5.33
       1024        7.68 s            133.4         154 MB    5.39
       2048       15.77 s            129.9         221 MB    5.56
       4096       32.80 s            124.9         355 MB    5.83
       7168       69.33 s            103.4         557 MB    6.27
   Tokenizing a history is milliseconds. Process memory grows by 276 MB at load and peaks at 914 MB in every row.

3. Moving the state (temporary probe on the cuda crate, DeviceKv::export_page / import_page and Gpu::download / upload,
   best of 3; the probe was deleted afterwards)
     tokens   state      device->host   host->device
       512     83 MiB      29.6 ms        11.6 ms
      1024    115 MiB      24.7 ms        14.3 ms
      2048    179 MiB      38.2 ms        20.7 ms
      4096    307 MiB      63.6 ms        32.3 ms
      8192    563 MiB     124.5 ms        60.2 ms
   SipHash (the crate's content hash) runs at 7.7 GB/s in memory: 77 ms for 563 MiB. The 4.35 GB of weights hash in 0.98 s
   (page cache; 4.4 GB/s). The config+tokenizer hash takes 3 ms.
   NVMe (dd, 1 MiB blocks): write+fsync 117 MiB 85 ms, 307 MiB 232 ms, 563 MiB 716 ms, 2.1 GiB 1.49 s; cold read
   (O_DIRECT) 51 ms, 130 ms, 259 ms, 869 ms; cached read 10, 21, 56, 249 ms.

4. Estimated total at 8,192 tokens: save = 125 + 77 + 716 ms + 1 s weight hash = 1.9 s; restore = 259 + 77 + 60 ms + 1 s = 1.4 s,
   against 80 s of replay. At 1,024 tokens: restore ~1.1 s against 7.7 s. Every resume of a saved session pays only the restore;
   one save pays back on the first resume above ~150 tokens (replay 7.5 ms per token vs a fixed ~1 s).

5. Measured end to end (15-checkpoint-e2e.sh; chat; /checkpoint took 1.03 s at 495 tokens; at 2,265 tokens exporting and writing 202 MB took 0.11 s, plus the ~1 s weight hash; ctx 4096, temperature 0, one question after resume, model load included)
     history    resume from text    resume from checkpoint    restore    save (/checkpoint)
      495 tok       11.48 s              8.81 s                 1.03 s    1.03 s
     1382 tok       18.37 s              9.06 s                 1.05 s    -
     2265 tok       25.53 s              9.23 s                 1.06 s    -
   Answers identical every time. Of the 1.06 s restore, 0.98 s is hashing the weight files and ~0.09 s reading, checking and
   uploading 202 MB. 2,265-token run repeated: 25.59 / 25.62 s vs 9.37 / 9.34 s.
   Peak memory (process RSS, nvidia-smi VRAM at 0.1 s): resume from text 871.3 MiB / 5,491 MiB, from checkpoint 871.6 MiB /
   5,491 MiB (idle 468 MiB). The restore streams one block at a time: no host buffer the size of the file, and the old cache is
   released before the new one is filled.
   Real code session: 3 tool calls, resumed from another directory; checkpoint path is the same (mode code).

6. Numerical gates and what was measured (crates/model/tests/qwen35_state.rs, crates/cli/tests/checkpoint.rs, checkpoint_cli.rs)
   - Exported and imported cache continues bit for bit like the original (tiny fixture: prefixes 31, 64, 65, 130; Qwen3.5-4B-Q8:
     200-token prefix + 16-token tail). Versus a one-pass replay of the same tokens a logit moves by at most 3.4e-8 on the
     fixture: that rounding comes from reading a prompt in two passes (also without a checkpoint), not from the checkpoint.
   - Any single changed byte at 9 positions, 6 truncations, a trailing byte, 7 kinds of mismatch (revision, session, mode, model,
     weights, precision, configuration), a context that is too small, an empty cache and a cache that must be replayed are
     all refused; the session's cache stays empty and usable.
   - Through the binary: a damaged, a half-length and an older-revision checkpoint give a note and the same answer as a plain
     resume; /save removes the checkpoint it outdated; /checkpoint refuses unsaved, changed, short (<256 tokens) and non-GPU cases.

7. Decision: GO for an opt-in prototype, Qwen3.5 on CUDA only. /checkpoint is separate from /save; nothing but text is kept
   otherwise. Limits: 256 tokens minimum, 1 GiB per file, 4 GiB in all. The largest single cost left is the weight-file
   hash (about 1 s per restore and per checkpoint); computing it while the model loads, as --snapshot does, would remove it.
   Not done: any other model or backend, restoring a prefix of a checkpoint, sharing checkpoints between sessions.

Reproduce: 15-checkpoint-e2e.sh 6 (needs MARIN_QWEN35_Q8_DIR or ~/models/Qwen3.5-4B-Q8); the replay table with
marin-run bench as above; the opt-in tests with MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 MARIN_QWEN35_Q8_DIR=... cargo test -p model --test qwen35_state
and cargo test -p cli --test checkpoint --test checkpoint_cli.
